ary\":\"The implicated cellular lineage links lymphoid progenitor cells to lymphoblasts in ALL pathogenesis framing\",\"explanation\":\"Lymphoid progenitor cells are described as the progenitor lineage that gives rise to lymphoblasts, and this developmental progression is explicitly represented by relationships connecting progenitors to lymphoblasts. This lineage connection supplies biological context for how ALL is framed as arising from lymphoid progenitor cells that become malignant and yield excessive blasts. [Data: Entities (918, 919, 2563); Relationships (1372, 4807)]\\n\\nThe community’s descriptions repeatedly highlight lymphoid progenitor cells as central to the origin and pathogenesis of ALL and note they may be increased in the context of ALL pathogenesis. While not adding external molecular detail, the network makes clear that the progenitor→blast relationship is a core explanatory scaffold for the disease. [Data: Entities (918, 2563, 919); Relationships (1372, 4703)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Abnormal white blood cells in this community are specifically identified as lymphoblasts\",\"explanation\":\"“Abnormal white blood cells” is defined here as disease-associated white blood cells present in excessive numbers in ALL, and the relationship from ALL to abnormal white blood cells specifies that the abnormal cells are lymphoblasts. This makes the term a synonym/clinical label for blasts in the context of ALL rather than a broad category. [Data: Entities (3058, 919, 2563); Relationships (5989)]\\n\\nBy tying the abnormal WBC framing to a specific cell type (lymphoblasts), the community supports interpretation of lab/clinical language in a way that is directly relevant to diagnosis and monitoring described elsewhere (bone marrow blast percentage). [Data: Entities (3058, 919); Relationships (4867, 5989)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Specialized clinical expertise (ALL specialist) is positioned as important for care decisions\",\"explanation\":\"An “ALL specialist” is defined as